    - How to deal with interannual variabitility is an open question with no real good solution
    - winter and summer varaibility are very different and not necessarily in phase
    - May need to have different treatments for different season
    - May need to have different treatments for event detection and data for HT fitting
    - This will all take quite a bit of time with no guarantee of uptake by the MHW community
    - It also will be very complicated
    - So perhaps at this point it would be best to focus on extended summer only
    - but still many options to consider
    - I think we will have to have both simple MS with linear trend & s(doy) 
    - and something more complex, with some form of interannual variability term

# 2025-11-20
    - v_hobday == fixed with year annual cycle 90th ptile  : uses doExtractEvents_Hobday.R
    - v1       == v_hobday with linear trend with time     : uses doExtractEvents_Hobday_wtime.R
    - v2       ==v_hobday with linear trend with GMST      : uses doExtractEvents_Hobday_wtime.R

      # v_Hobday: match Hobday as best we can, event threshold 0.90, climC term linear with time
      uses doExtractEvents_Hobday.R
      ms.k              <- list(doy=12, gmst=4)
      fmla.MSqgam       <- list(x ~ stime  +s(sdoy, bs='cc',k=ms.k$doy)  , ~ s(sdoy))
      chosen.MSgpd.name <- "Hobday" #  best match to hobday
      clim.year         <- 1996  # middle year of climatology period

      v1: as v_Hobday but allowing linear trend with time but fixed annual cycle
      ms.k              <- list(doy=12, gmst=4)
      fmla.MSqgam       <- list(x ~ stime  +s(sdoy, bs='cc',k=ms.k$doy)  , ~ s(sdoy))
      chosen.MSgpd.name <- "Hobday" #  best match to hobday
      clim.year         <- NULL  # no fixed climatology

      # v2: as for v1 but allowing linear trend with GMST but fixed annual cycle
      ms.k              <- list(doy=12, gmst=4)
      fmla.MSqgam       <- list(x ~ gmst  +s(sdoy, bs='cc',k=ms.k$doy)  , ~ s(sdoy))
      chosen.MSgpd.name <- "DLG" # doy + linear gmst
      clim.year         <- NULL  # no fixed climatology


